A||||57||TheFiero64||Who wears there seatbelt?||||5||
Z||000000||TheFiero64||01-03-2001||02:27 PM||||After looking at that crash story that said all 5 people were not wearing there seatbelts I was just wondering who wears and dose not wear there seatbelt? I always wear mine. I feel unprotected without mine. I was in a rollover accident about 5 years ago and would have been thrown if I had not had mine on.<P>------------------<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||01-03-2001||02:34 PM||||I wear mine. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||kevin||01-03-2001||02:46 PM||||I only put mine on when I see 'the man' behind me or perhaps cruising next to me on the freeway. Otherwise, I get lazy unless I find myself attacking the apexes, then of course, I think it is overall quite prudent.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Greg Piet||01-03-2001||02:50 PM||||I wear mine and if I had a harness I would slap that on as well...<P>I am of the opinion all cars shouldnt have leather and wood interior, but a full rollcage off the assembly line though.||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||01-03-2001||02:50 PM||||I wear mine so that I'll stay in the seat while taking turns.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  But, for some reason, I always forget to put it on when I'm not the one driving!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||01-03-2001||02:52 PM||||I allways buckle up even before I start the car. Otherwise the blue box chimes at me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||01-03-2001||02:54 PM||||I always wear mine - I've been a passenger in two nasty accidents and suffered only a knot on the side of the head.<P>I used to keep a little slack in the belt.  But I took a high-performance driving class at Mid-Ohio once in my 84 Coupe, carried too much speed into a turn and went off into the grass.  I hit a bump in the grass, the car got air, my a$$ came out of the seat and my helmet hit hard enough to dent the headliner.  Guess the seatbelts don't work unless they're tight, huh?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I guess that's why guys who get serious about racing use a harness.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||01-03-2001||03:26 PM||||I don't wear mine and have had friends in crashes that would have been killed had they been wearing 'safety' belts!<P>One was rounding a corner when a semi came from the other direction on the wrong side of the road. He and his passenger dove for the floor board. The top of the car was removed as well as the seatbacks. No way they would have survived.<P>As in everything, you have the 'majority' out there barking and making noise about people that would be alive had they had on their belts. Just because there is not as much noise from families of people that got killed because of their belts.... But that is another story...<P>My main gripe is that it should not be a point of legislation. The government sticks their nose in places where it don't belong for one of two reasons- money from lobbies, or just to control us. It hurts no one but me if I choose not to wear a seat belt. It is my choice. If it were pressed, they would find that the gov't has no Constitution grounds to enforce such a law.<P>I pay my $10 fine each time, and then I tell them on the spot that it don't matter, they have no right to make me wear it. Like helmet laws, eventually it will be changed back...<P>And that brings my next point... The gov't says we have to wear seatbelts because SIG's are whining about people dying because of not wearing them. What next? Some study will show that people are dying from head injuries in automobile accident? Are they gonna make us wear helmets in our cars? Or people are getting burned up in fiery car crashes... Fire suits in our cars?<P>Two more things that sort of relate to this overstepping of power.... Air bags - made to save lives. Now people hate them because they kill. But they are still there and can't be cut off on most cars. Catalytic convertors - Made to save the enviroment. Now we know that the fumes from catalytic convertors actually depletes the ozone. And we also know that a finely tuned engine that is in good running order burns a lot cleaner than a car with a cat on it....<P>I love my country, but my government scares me. Maybe they'll start worrying more about John Q. Public instead of big money lobbies one day.. who knows?<P>That's what I think about seatbelts (and a few other things)...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Hank is Here||01-03-2001||03:28 PM||||I wear my seatbelt all the time in my Fiero and other modern cars.  Other than that my old jeeps that don't have rollbars or tops, I don't cause I would rather be thrown than crushed.||||reg||1
Z||000009||jerpro||01-03-2001||03:43 PM||||I wear my seatbelt, and will be buying a five point seat soon.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||RobertVol||01-03-2001||04:19 PM||||Since this a case were most people will diagree I have to do the opposite.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Seriously though I really do agree with Songman on this. This is a decision that should be handled by the owner of the vehicle. We dont need big brother telling us when to wear seatbelts. Now if it affected other drivers THATS one thing but it does not. <P>This law to me is as stupid as having a lawsuit were smokers can get money for not knowing that smoking was not healthy~ !! PLEASE and I smoke ! oops .......<P>Hope that doesnt start another one!! <P>BTW to make Fiero related. I DONT WEAR mine in my GT!!! <P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=819451&a=5959473&p=36934984&Sequence=1"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||loafer87gt||01-03-2001||04:37 PM||||Its the first thing I do when I get in the car. When I was younger and riding with my older brother he always used to slam on the brakes when I didn't have my belt on. After wacking my melon off the dash several times, I learned my lesson and always wear my belt. I remember reading back in article about a year back that the majority of Canadians wear their belts, whereas our neighbour south of the border seem to dislike wearing them, and refuse to buckle up. Probably due to the fact that us Canucks seem to just oblige and do whatever the government tells us without question, whereas you Americans seem to be quite vigilant in defending your personal rights.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mshill||01-03-2001||04:45 PM||||Songman, I have to agree on most points.  Here's one more for you... California puts MTBE in the gasoline at an enourmous cost to the consumers, just to find out that it pollutes the water supply.  The real kicker is that you have to burn 10% more fuel because it is inefficient and it only reduced the pollutants by 10% so it was a wash for air pollution and a loss for the water supply.  But can we get the stuff out of our gasoline, no way!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||01-03-2001||04:52 PM||||I always wear my seatbelt on public roads. I agree that seatbelt use should not be law. But, I give little to no sympathy to those who are injured or killed because they wouldn't wear a belt. Every now and then on the news they report on a car accident where people die because they weren't wearing their seatbelt, and would be alive had they been wearing it. Just the other week they reported about 5 teenagers dying in a wreck because they didn't wear their seat belts. Those 5 kids would be alive if they had used their sestbelts. They had a shot at life, they blew it, tough luck.<BR>I know will probably get lots of flack about my view on this, but that's where I am on this situtation.<P>Recap, wear your belts if you want, it shouldn't be law, but its your own fault if your injured because you wouldn't wear a belt.<P>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-03-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Alex2004||01-03-2001||04:56 PM||||I put mine on before i start the car!||||reg||10
Z||000015||KRMFiero||01-03-2001||05:24 PM||||I always ware mine, i would never get in a car if it didnt have seat belts, i have known too many people that died from accendents (plus i am on a volinteree fire dept. and i have seens some pretty bad stuff when people dont use seatbelts)<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<P>  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||TheFiero64||01-03-2001||05:25 PM||||Even though I do always wear mine and tell others it is a good idea, I don't think it should be the law||||reg||1
Z||000017||FieroMaster88||01-03-2001||05:32 PM||||I Always wear mine, always have.  It goes on before the car starts, even when I am not driving.  Doesnt bother me too much.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1.jpg"> <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster86<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles<BR>84 Indy Fiero (restoration Project)||||reg||1
Z||000018||Tigger||01-03-2001||05:34 PM||||I wear mine all the time, regardless that it's a law here in Michigan.  Sometimes I'll even catch myself putting it on just move cars around the driveway.<P>I was a passenger in a 45-50 mph wreck a few years ago when a drunk driver didn't make a stop.  I was thrown up against the belt.  No doubt without it, I would have gone head first through the windshield with a good chance of being run over.  Even if I didn't go completely through the windshield, I know my legs would have been busted up against the dash.  Anyway to make it short, I walked away.<P>About going under the trailers of 18 wheelers...  I don't know if many people know this, but you can still duck with a seatbelt on.  Try it next time you try to reach for something on the passenger side of the car.<P>Well, I walked away once and I'll keep taking those few chances with my belt on.<P>  BTW, the reason why the government was pressured to mandate airbags to be standard on all cars was to reduce the risk of injuries of those who didn't wear seatbelts.<P>That should be enough for many to chew on.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Raydar||01-03-2001||05:39 PM||||Seat belt (and helmet) laws suck.<P>Having said that, I always wear my seatbelt, and insist that passengers in my car do the same. <BR>This, ever since a seatbelt stopped me from  eating the dashboard, when a GP turned in front of me while I was driving a Monza. Pushed the GP about 6 feet sideways.<P>And I always wear a helmet when I'm on a bike. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Bob Englert||01-03-2001||05:46 PM||||I always wear my belt.  While I tend to agree that it should not be mandated for drivers over 18 or 21, you are always playing the odds when behind the wheel of a car.  I think the odds are much better to be buckled when involved in an accident.<P>Personally I would like to see better insurance rates or better coverage for drivers wearing their seatbelts comparied to those that elect not to wear them.  That way the person that doesn't want to wear them doesn't have to, but I don't have to pay fro their higher likelyhood of death or serious injury.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000021||BOILERMAKER||01-03-2001||06:00 PM||||I wear mine all the time no matter what car I am in unless I am not in one of the front seats.||||reg||1
Z||000022||hugh||01-03-2001||07:23 PM||||I wear mine all the time no matter what vehicle I'm in wether I'm driving or not.This started when my oldest daughter wouldn't let me in her car unless I wore my belt.Now I don't feel comfortable without it.In NJ you can now be stopped and ticketed for not wearing one.When my kids were younger I didn't realise how important belts were and they would stand on the back floor of the car between the bucket seats.I'm thankful I didn't have any accidents then.Now it really bothers me when I see someone driving with kids in their car not buckled up.||||reg||1
Z||000023||My7Fieros||01-03-2001||08:03 PM||||I do.....always.And to those of you who got into this long disertation about how its safer <I>not</I> to wear them...I'll come visit you in the hospital or your funeral when you get into a serious accident...||||reg||14
Z||000024||Cheever2||01-03-2001||08:14 PM||||(1)-My wreck happened so quickly I had no time to prepare for it.  Not even a quick prayer.<BR>(2)-I heard a veteran Trooper of many years once say he never pulled a dead body out of a seat belt.<BR>(3)-From all the studies I've seen, if they can be believed, it seems the vast majority of car injuries is to the feet & legs, not the head.<BR>(4)-If I die in a crash, I would rather leave my family saying "Well at least he was using his seat belt"; instead of saying "He didn't use his seat belt".<BR>(5)-I DON'T BELIEVE THE GOVERNMENT HAS ANY BUSINESS legislating anything that can be called saving us from ourselves.  I agree with Songman all the way on that.<BR>(6)-When I'm a passenger I don't wear it because they can be so difficult to put on I'm afraid I'll pull a muscle.  <P>I'm a fatalist anyway: your time is going to come when it is meant to.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000025||Fiero5||01-03-2001||08:36 PM||||I used to wear mine pretty regularly. Then, in 1985 I had a bit of an accident while working for Sears. I was making one of my standard service van runs in a Sears service/delivery van. For some weird reason on the way home from Springfield VT that October night, I didn't put my seat belt on on the way home. I had a blue sedan driving funny during the trip back to NH, and as he was passing me for the third time, I looked over to get a closer look at this dimwit. We were traveling a good 65 to 70 miles an hour. What I neglected to see as we rounded a left handed corner, was a tractor trailer had broken down and was sitting dead right in my lane. No flares, no signs, no nothin'. I never saw it.<P>I hit it so hard, I pushed a parked tractor  trailer right into the shoulder and over the side of the bank. The Sears van folded up and was crushed like a soda can under a heavy boot. All the items that I was shuttling in the back of the van became missils shooting to the front of the van like bullets. As I bounced around the front cabin like a loose doll, a space heater shot into the windshield right past my head. A tool box took out the passenger seat back as if it was styrofoam. A lamp shot into the back of the drivers seat like a javelin. I wasn't in the drivers seat at the moment, due to my being bounced around. <BR>The battery exploded on impact and the battery acid sprayed all over me. As windshield glass particles went all over the van cabin and in my face and eyes, I was still able to watch the roof of the van being peeled back like a sardeen can being quickly opened up. The rear of the tractor truck was up high enough to come smashing right through the van windshiled and peel half the roof back. I thought at first I had hit a house. It felt like every organ in my body had stopped and the world had exploded all around me. Even though we were going over the side of the bank as one hunk of metal fused to another, I thought the world had crashed to a stop.<P>I still have some very impressive photos that were taken of the van after the truck and van had been seperated. A service tech friend of mine even took a chilling shot of the van, and had it enlarged and framed for me.<P>The VT State Police arrived later with the accident report for me at the hospital as the doctors tried to get the glass out of my eyes. My parents were their and my mother was obviously very upset, so they told her to sit down before giving us the report. They had figured that their was at least 7 different ways I should have died, including decapitation. Except that I was obviously not wearing my seatbelt. I got points off my license for that :o)<P>I have a hard time even now when I decide at certain times that I should put my seatbelt on, or like the time in OOB when we were crusing around and the local officials were trying to get everyone to buckle up, due to the fact that if I had put it on back then, I wouldn't be alive today. It also still bothers me when I see another vehicle parked or broken down on the side of the road, where they are still very close or even somewhat still in the road, especially trucks!<P>Oh well, I'm done.||||reg||1
Z||000026||MrPBody||01-03-2001||08:36 PM||||I wear seat belts habitually.<P>While I agree philosophically that seat belt laws -- and other laws that protect us from ourselves -- are not necessary or desirable, seat belt laws don't bother me much because evidence shows that seat belts <B>do</B> work.  (As I interpret evidence.  Not everyone agrees, and I have no problem with that.)  I think there are plenty of other, dumber laws to complain about.<P>I prefer fixed belts (non-intertia-reel) because they hold my fanny firmly in the seat during crazy maneuvers.  I might even install a harness.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||2xsess||01-03-2001||08:37 PM||||I never used to wear mine, a few months driving in south FL changed that. <P>I got pulled over by an MP(Military Police) one moring because I had taken mine off to put my wallet in my pocket.<P>I don't think it should be a law. I do agree with helmet laws though. I was totally shocked when FL overturned theirs.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000028||Cliff Pennock||01-03-2001||09:00 PM||||I always wear my seatbelt and it has saved my life in a car crash once. I also always insist that my passenger wears it.<P>I've heared of the "he was saved because he <B>wasn't</B> wearing his seatbelt"-stories. But they are <B>far</B> outweighed by the "he was saved because he <B>was</B> wearing his seatbelt"-stories. Simple math.||||reg||1
Z||000029||BAM-BAM||01-03-2001||09:06 PM||||  I have to disagree with Songman and the others. The fact that seat belts save lives is a fact! No, not in every instance, but far more lives are saved, and injuries overted by the use of seat belts. With that no one can argue. The fact that the government has stepped in is our fault. We as a people, as a society, choose to put others at fault even when we are to blame. By doing so we live in (once again) a victim laden and litigious society. The fact that  people sue over injuries both factual and imaginary is the reason for the laws. The fact that everybody pays for those injuries and lives lost (premiums) is the impetus behind the legislation.<BR>  No one likes to pay the rates they pay for insurance. If rates were based on measures you as an individual employ to insure your safety, all of a sudden everyone would be "wearing" their seatbelts. At least they would say they did. If you honestly believe that not wearing a seatbelt is safer that wearing one, maybe you people should visit a morgue sometime and view the bodies of those thrown from vehicals. In California, when I was in highschool, we had to watch weekly videos from CHP and study the CHP periodical for drivers education. It was a true turning point for this sixteen year old. It was disgusting. That was where I heard the A California Highway Patrolman said that he had never unbelted a dead body. <BR>  The point being if we as adults, could be trusted to be responsible for ourselves and others, then laws like this would not be neccessary. But the sad fact is, that we as adults want to do what we want, when we want and to whom we want; then we blame  someone else and make others pay the price. I feel that you have the absolute right to not wear your seatbelt or helmet or practice any other safety measures. But when the time comes and you are the one thrown from the car, or doing a face slide down the highway, then I believe your insurance company should have the right to deny your claim for failure to employ available and recommended safety equipment. Then "YOU" can pay the bills, or your families can start a local trust in your name to pay your hospital of funeral expenses. I get tired of having to be resposible for the ignorance of others. If you all cant see where seatbelts and helmets and airbags save lives and lessen injuries, then GOD help you when the time comes. <BR>  Oh by the way, I wear my belt every time I'm in a vehical and my passengers will wear theirs or they won't be my passengers.<P> Peace, B2 OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P> P.S. I'm not advocating government interference, I just think we should all be a little more self regulated. Then we wouldn't need someone else to regulate us. Just good common sense!  <p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 01-03-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000030||Fformula88||01-03-2001||09:45 PM||||I always have the belt on.  I also want everyone in my car to have one on.  I have an aunt who was thrown out of a sunroof in an accident where she didn't have a seatbelt on.  She lived, but with very serious brain damage and has never been the same person again.  Thats enough to convinve me to wear it!  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000031||BN Boomer||01-03-2001||10:04 PM||||I don't really believe the government has any business mandating seat belt or helmet use, either. But I also don't believe the government should come along and use my tax dollars to clean up the mess and take care of some idiot after he's cracked his noggin' open as direct result of him making a conscious decision to not wear a seat belt or helmet or drive in an irresponsible manner. So until you are left to take care of yourself as a result of your own stupidity rather than expecting me to cough up the money through continued high taxes, I don't really have that big of a problem with seat belt or helmet laws. <P>Additionally, some of you guys may not realize the risk you are putting yourself at as a result of choosing to not wear a seat belt or helmet. It's my understanding that insurance companies often get out having to pay for your medical care or otherwise compensate you when your injuries are able to be attributed to to your own negligence through not wearing a seat belt, regardless of who is found to be at fault in the accident.<P>I wear my seat belt all of the time and insist that everyone else in the vehicle wear theirs as well. At work, not wearing a seat belt in a company vehicle will bring swift disciplinary action, not to mention that you will have a difficult time getting them to compensate you should you be unfortunate enough to be involved in an accident while traveling in a vehicle belonging to the company or one of it's contract haulers.<P>As far as airbags go, I was reading that so far they have contributed to the death of something 150 people but have saved the lives of 5,000. And airbag technology is being developed and refined as we speak so that in the future they will offer even greater protection while at the same time being safer for everyone.||||reg||1
Z||000032||wkayl||01-03-2001||10:10 PM||||Thank God for seatbelts.  Here was a 40mph crash my son and I had last fall...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/OOPS.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||FieroLisa||01-03-2001||10:45 PM||||I usually wear my seatbelt, but not as much as I should. Always in the Fiero, but when I';m in the Tracer I never do the lapbelt since it's got automatic shoulder ones. BUt that's what caused my back problems in a 40 mph headon crash. Haven't I learned yet?<BR>Also when riding my horses, I wear a helmet at my lessons where I ride fairly sane horses. Yet when I ride my own skitzo spawn of satan Thoroughbred, I never do. This summer when I was riding her, she flipped out and fell over, on top of me. Good thing she landed only on my leg. Oh well.<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 gold 4 speed GT<BR>86 gold 5 speed SC (For Sale)<BR>Columbus, OH/Greensboro NC<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<BR>*As Always, Updated Website*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000034||Songman||01-03-2001||10:59 PM||||The point I was making stands out in this forum. Everyone makes their choice. Some of us choose not to wear them even though it is the law. Some would wear them even if it wasn't.<P>There are times when I will wear one. Bad weather in a Fiero will scare anyone into putting a seat belt on!<P>And I mentioned helmet laws. I have my helmet. I would never cruise down the interstate without it on. But it was not all that popular having to sit in Daytona at Bike Week in stop and go traffic with a helmet on. The most you could do would be to lay your bike over.<P>And there is a story about someone in my family on this one too. Back in the 70s, my cousins rode motorcycles. The whole family - Dad, Mom, kids. Everyone had a bike. The mom and dad decided to take a trip one weekend and decided to only take one bike. Both wore helmets.<P>When the car pulled out in front of them there was nothing they could do. Dad was killed instantly because he went head first into the car. Mom would probably have been okay other than bruises or broken bones because she was thrown over the hood. But fate was not on her side this day. Her helmet caught on something and the chin strap peeled up across her face taking everything with it. She didn't die until a few years later but she laid in a hospital room as a vegetable with only machines keeping her alive. Gruesome story I know, but when an accident happens you never know which way it could go. <P>One of my good friends was just recently killed on his bike too. He was a motorcycle cop here and was chasing a speeder when a lady pulled out in front of him. Motorcycles are dangerous. In anything other than the most minor accident you are probably dead, helmet or not.<P>I'm not saying anyone is right or wrong. I just want my choice. All of you are my buddies and you have the same right to choose as I do.<P>Sorry these are so long.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000035||frontal lobe||01-04-2001||12:25 AM||||I ALWAYS wear my seatbelt.<P>I'm not for seatbelt laws.<P>I HATE the stupid retracting seat belts.  They get in the way, and you still have to manually do the lap belt.  And to FieroLisa who probably already knows, you NEED the lap belt, too.<P>To Cheever2 and all other fatalists who say "Your time is going to come when it is meant to."   Please consider this:  yes, when it's your time, you can't go LONGER.  However, you can go SOONER than "your time".  Examples:  suicide, drug abuse, alcoholism, etc.  So maybe it wasn't somebody's time, but they didn't have their seatbelt on.<P>One more thing.  It's funny how intuitively we put our seatbelts on at times we feel most vulnerable like aggressive driving, etc.  But we are just as much or more in danger just puttering around and getting nailed by someone else.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000036||DaRkLoRD||01-04-2001||03:06 AM||||I used to hang out with a girl who refused to wear her seatbelt. Here, if I get caught with anyone in my car not wearing a seatbelt, I get severely nailed.. points off my license, possibly a fine, etc.<BR>I always had to force her to put her belt on.. but a few times she took it off as soon as I wasn't looking anymore, so I told her I wouldn't drive with her unless she was wearing it.<P>I agree, being able to duck in the case of something hitting your seat which could impale or decapitate you would be a plus... but I don't see a way around that as long as you're strapped in to protect against other dangers. I want to get a racing harness eventually, if/when I get racing seats.. and I guess I'll have to take my chances with other dangers. As long as cars and drivers are the way they are right now, there's no way to be 100% safe around them..<P>I won't ever argue that speed limits are unfair, in fact I think they're too high in residential areas. (a kid was hit by a car in front of my house last summer) While I don't think things like seatbelts should be required by law, it should affect a person's insurance.. <BR>Same with drinking and smoking, for their medical insurance. I do NOT think a person who is dying from lung cancer due to smoking, should get government funded health care for their treatment.. but the Canadian gov (to my knowledge) doesn't discriminate on things like this.<P>but anyway, to answer the question.. I wear my seatbelt. always.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000037||RaeQwan||01-04-2001||03:33 AM||||Songman, could you put your two posts together to make sorta a 'article' for me to post on my site? my site needs it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe<P><BR><A HREF="http://www.WebJolt.net" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Network</A><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwanegg.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000038||Racer 1||01-04-2001||04:04 AM||||I was in one accident in my fiero going 35 mph. I was coming into town going 35-40 mph and this scumbag decides to stop in the middle lane and let a COP into a parking lot. Me and my friend were looking at the radio and I glanced up to see this dirtbag about 20 yards or so out infront. I slammed on the brakes and the back brakes locked up but the front didnt. In a way it was a cool feeling like I was in slow motion all the way up into impact which I estimated was about 10-15 mph. Me and my friend were fine we had on your seatbelts on. We got out of the car talked to the guy I had hit and agreed to just go our seperate ways without any COPS getting involved. Only spidered the paint on the front nose piece HEHE. I think the positives of using seatbelts out way the negitives. I really hate these parents that have there young 4-7 year old kids not buckeled up and hanging out the rear windows. And mothers holding babies in there laps in the front seat. :O<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://spitfire.cwv.net/~cdbailey/images/gifssucl/eyes2/evil_eyes.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000039||tgowens||01-04-2001||04:13 AM||||I wear my seatbelt <I>most</I> of the time....It is funny tho that we (most of us) tend to wear them on the highway but if we fail to wear it it is usually on a three block trip to the 7-11....but - accidents usually happen within ten miles of home? -- guess we'd all better buckle up on the short trips too!<P>I agree with many of Songman's points about legislation and rights of choice.....now, here is another one for you to think about...the law says that if you own property and someone encroaches on that property that it is considered trespassing, right? ok, here is the scenario......you own your car, there is a license plate on that car (required by law) that the government has placed (or caused to be placed there) and you don't want it on your car (they have trespassed, right?) - now, why <I>is</I> it there in the first place? To make it legal to drive on the highway - now, <I>whose</I> highway IS it? Since you pay the taxes and the state builds the highway it technically is <I>your</I> highway.........so, we allow the state to break a law by trespassing on our property in order to display a tag we don't want in order that we may drive on our own highway.......<P>Like I said..........something else to think about............||||reg||1
Z||000040||Jaygee79||01-04-2001||12:15 PM||||I always wear mine.  It's become a habit.  I feel weird not wearing one now, especially if i'm driving.  But if I'm in the back seat I don't usually wear it.||||reg||1
Z||000041||Songman||01-04-2001||12:30 PM||||I agree with you totally, Terry! And they also tell us that driving is a benefit, not a right. Who gave them the power to give us this benefit?<P>The bottom line is that they are going to do whatever they want and most of us will just sit around and take it. We will whine but we will take it.<P>Sure, there has to be some system to stop maniacs, but I don't have any ideas. I just think gov't oversteps it's boundaries an awful lot.<P>One more story of seatbelts of not... Several years ago I had finally gotten my dream car. Brand new 911 Porsche Targa. Red with tan! I loved it! I was home for Christmas visiting my family in GA. I was approaching an intersection that was three lanes going each way. I was in the far right lane and there was no one in front of me up to the light. at about 20 yards the light which had been red turned green. I was going between 30-40 when I got under the light. The idiot in the turning lane on the other side decided he was going to try to beat traffic and punched acros three lanes!<P>He made the first two and then there was me! Like someone described earlier, it was like slow motion. I slammed the brakes and I'm sitting here thinking 'I am going to wreck my car!'. When he saw me, instead of punching it he slammed on the brakes in his junky old Ford Granada. He stopped dead in front of me! Nothing more I could do.. T-bone! I spun and took out the drivers side of my car as well.<P>Anyway.. I wasn't wearing my seat belt obviously. Not a scratch. As I said earlier, in a wreck, you never know.<P>The idiot that caused the wreck just stood there saying, 'That sure was a pretty little car. Good think I was in my wife's car instead of my 4x4 truck. You would have gone under it.' That is where having the seatbelt on would have gotten me.<P>I miss my car. They totalled it.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000042||Songman||01-04-2001||12:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RaeQwan:<BR><B>Songman, could you put your two posts together to make sorta a 'article' for me to post on my site? my site needs it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>RaeQwan, feel free to copy and paste if you want. You can edit for space if you need to.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000043||TheFiero64||01-04-2001||12:37 PM||||I just want to slap all the stupid people that cause accidents!!!||||reg||1
Z||000044||Vlad||01-05-2001||08:01 AM||||Guys<P>I happened to be a nurse in Emergency Room of huge hospital. You know...... like ER on TV........))<P>So each case of multitraumas was followed up to the point what car and tires patient had at the time of accident. And, of course, seatbelt was encountered.....<P>So my point is, DO use your seatbelts. They REALLY save lives in 95% of major accidents.<BR>And they may kill you in 5% of the rest.<P>So, what chances  do you take? It's up to you.....||||reg||1
Z||000045||Mi Junk||01-05-2001||10:53 PM||||I wear my seat belt, in the front seats but very rarly in the back. Here in Ontario it is I think a $132 fine plus 2 points. 15 points means no more driving for at least 6 months. <BR>And to really top it off if a child under 16 the driver gets the fine and point for each kid unbelted. ||||reg||1
Z||000046||RobertVol||01-06-2001||12:16 AM||||You guys are probably right I should wear it. <P>I guess I am just to stubborn <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Call it the Fiero Right!!<P>But really we should all wear them but My point was again the point that the government thinks they should babysit us. I hate it everytime they pay politicians and law enforcment TAX money to enforce Babysitting rules. <P>To the point that our money going to the bills of medical coverage for people who dont wear their belts well.....I totally have to disagree that is a major point. Now if you said our insurance rates rise for the monies being paid out then maybe yeah but then: <P>1. Smokers<BR>2. Overeating causing heartattacks<BR>3. And people who just frankly dont take care of themselves <P>I think these three things cause more claims to be filed. and they would cause a bigger increase in health care coverage. More than seatbelts......<P>So MAYBE we should make it a law that you can only <P>1. Visit Mcdonalds once a week or get ticket<BR>2. If caught smoking more than a 1/2 a pack you get fined<BR>3. Everyone will report to the gym on Saturday of each month or you will have your license SUSPENDED!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Now after all that Blabbering <P>Point is :<P>Belts are safer and we should wear them just not be babysitted to wear them..<P>Thanks for the space. <P><P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=819451&a=5959473&p=36934984&Sequence=1"> ||||reg||1
Z||000047||2M4 Dale||01-06-2001||12:38 AM||||ALWAYS, ALWAYS, ALWAYS...since about 1975 when I was into street racing my 67 Goat !!!||||reg||4
Z||000048||William Federle||01-06-2001||07:11 AM||||I wear mine whenever I drive the car (no one else drives it).||||reg||1
Z||000049||Old Lar||01-06-2001||10:28 AM||||I always wear mine.  It holds me to the seat.  However I agree that the government should not legislate safety rules.  Helmet laws and  seat belts laws should not be on the books.<P>Darwin's laws will take care of those who want to do their own thing and not use supplied safety features.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smashed_1.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000050||TheFiero64||01-06-2001||10:39 AM||||Old Lar, What happened to that GT. Was the driver all right?||||reg||5
Z||000051||Songman||01-06-2001||10:56 AM||||Does anyone supposed that a seatbelt would have saved that person? I doubt it...<P>That is awful! You hate to see stuff like that. It really hits you in the guy when you see a car that looks like yours all torn up like that...<P>There has been a lot of talk about this subject. And I don't suppose that anyone is right or wrong. Everyone makes their way through life as best he can. I certainly don't wish any harm on anyone for not agreeing with how I choose to lead my life. If I tried to push my way on others that would make me a hypocrite, wouldn't it?<P>I want everyone to be safe and personally can't stand the thought of anyone getting hurt in any way. For me, I'll take defensive and sane driving as my safety net. Of course, there is always the other person that you can't control, but no matter what we do we can't control that.<P>Personally, I just choose not to speed, not to tailgate, keep my safety area around my car, just use common sense and not think that this vehicle that I am in is some sort of weapon for highway use. It has worked well for me so far. People who don't do this are the reason you see those people lying in the emergency room! And the sad thing is, it is usually not the people that were acting stupid that end up there. It is the innocent guy that was caught up in the other person's act.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000052||Old Lar||01-06-2001||11:09 AM||||The driver walked away to buy an 88 Formula.  The GT only had 8700 miles on it.||||reg||14
Z||000053||KnightRyder31||01-06-2001||11:32 AM||||I always wear mine...its the law, and it keeps me safe. And I don't care about the people who say other wise.<BR>I agree with My7fiero's all the way.||||reg||1
Z||000054||StuGood||01-06-2001||03:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>I don't wear mine and have had friends in crashes that would have been killed had they been wearing 'safety' belts!<P>One was rounding a corner when a semi came from the other direction on the wrong side of the road. He and his passenger dove for the floor board. The top of the car was removed as well as the seatbacks. No way they would have survived...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds like a pretty dramatic news story... no doubt you have a copy of the article.  No matter.  Sounds like a rare situation in any case (you know, like the guy who says, "I always carry playing cards in my pocket - just in case somebody with a gun points it at me and says, "Give me the two of clubs, or I'll blow you away  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!"<P>Good grief - I thought seat belts would be a no-brainer by now.  I kind of agree that seat belt usage needn't be legislated.  The law doesn't bother me, because I use seat belts anyway.  It <B>did</B>, however, need to be legislated that cars have proper strengthening/mounting provisions for installing seat belts.  Not just anybody would / could go to the trouble of installing belts properly.  <P>Helmets are different, I guess, because anyone can buy a helmet and wear it - it's reasonably convenient to use them if one chooses to.  Of course, when I see someone riding a motorcycle w/o helmet, I sometimes think they don't value their own life much. <P>On the other hand, for some reason, I dislike airbags.  Probably because I feel like I'm driving around with an explosive in front of my face, and I haven't yet bothered to figure out how they work.  <P>Seat belts, on the other hand, are simple, cheap and ordinarily effective.  JMHO.||||reg||1
Z||000055||Songman||01-07-2001||08:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B> Sounds like a pretty dramatic news story... no doubt you have a copy of the article.  No matter.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is what always pisses me off about things like this... Ever since this thread started I have been very nice and respectful to other people who may not agree with my views. I have said repeatedly that I like all of you guys and that I would never try to force my ideals on anyone or I would be a hypocrite.<P>Now out of the blue, I am basically called a liar! This really pisses me off! It amazes me how supposedly grown people can't have a conversation where not everyone agrees with someone having to act like a child!<P>I think I'll just lurk for a while. Sorry, but as I said this kind of stuff really pisses me off!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> ||||reg||8
Z||000056||stimpy||01-07-2001||09:30 AM||||I personally wear my seatbelt 80% of the time. Prior to seabelt laws, I rarely if ever wore them. Sometimes it takes the threat of penalty of law in order to encouage compliance with a good idea.  For example, I know many people who don't do drugs, never touched the stuff. Upon investigating their rationale for abstaining, the majority will state "because it's illegal".  Not because it fries your brains, takes away your motivation and leaves you flat broke. It's illegal. So do the same individuals who decry seatbelt laws clamor for an end to the "War on drugs"?  Well, do you?<P>I know that my mom was in a head-on accident when I was in highschool. We were living in Europe at the time where there were very strict seatbelt laws. My family learned to religiously wear belts out of fear of the high fines.  But when my mom survived driving her Peugeot into a Alfa on a back road, I was very thankful for the law.||||reg||1
Z||000057||Joe4x4||01-07-2001||10:22 AM||||Hmmmmm  yes, no, sometimes.<P>I think the question is FREEDOM of CHOICE.<BR>Yes they do save lives and also are part of the few deaths .<P>Why stop at lap and shoulder staps, go with a helmit and don`t forget a fire suite ( incase of fire ) The cars must have a 12 point cage , 4-point seat belt.<P>I`ve been in accidents car,boat,motorcycle<BR>I used up 8 of my nine lives, still here.<P>What I`m saying is ...tell me the pro`s and con`s ...let me pick what I want to do .....<BR>DON`T FORSE me !!!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/vegasdieBLK.gif"> ||||reg||6
